# Shell scripts run under WSL and in containers, where a carriage return at the end of the shebang line makes
# the file unrunnable: the kernel looks for an interpreter literally named "bash\r". Git had been checking
# these out with Windows line endings, so every one of them failed with "/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file
# or directory" - a message that names neither the file nor the reason.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
docker/Dockerfile text eol=lf

# PowerShell runs on Windows, where CRLF is what it expects.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# **Binary files must not be line-ending converted.** Git decides "binary" by looking for a NUL byte in the
# first 8000 bytes, and a small encrypted PDF need not contain one - the 805-byte rc4-40.pdf does not. On a
# machine with core.autocrlf=false (a developer's) it is checked out untouched; on a Windows CI runner, where
# checkout sets autocrlf=true, every newline inside it is rewritten and the fixture no longer decrypts.
# That showed up as six failures in the PDF suite reading like a broken security handler - a crypto bug that
# was really a checkout bug, and one that could not reproduce on the machine it was written on.
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.wasm binary
